\title{Matching contracting shards give partial sums}
\taxon{Card}
\tag{card}
\meta{anki}{1786546736136}
\meta{anki-deck}{MLSys}

\prompt{[Sharded](XGI0) matmul, case 3: both multiplicands are sharded along the contracting dimension on the same mesh axis, #{A[I, J_X] \cdot B[J_X, K]}. What happens, and which primitive fixes it?}{
  The local shards \em{can} be multiplied — they share contracting indices — but each device ends up with only a \strong{partial sum}, written #{C[I, K]\{U_X\}}, "unreduced along #{X}":

  ##{A \cdot B = \sum_i A[:,i] \otimes B[i,:]}

  Device #{i} owns the #{i}-th column block of #{A} and the #{i}-th row block of #{B}, so it computes exactly the #{i}-th term of that sum.

  \strong{The fix} is [AllReduce](U351) along #{X} for a replicated #{C[I, K]} — or the cheaper #{\mathrm{ReduceScatter}_{X,K} \to C[I, K_X]} if you can leave the output sharded. That is [half the cost](MJ7A), and defers the gather, possibly forever.
}
